# Paper II General Ability Test, Part B General Knowledge, Section A Physics
# (the largest GK share, about 25 percent): properties of matter, units,
# motion, heat, sound, light, electricity and magnetism. Class 10 to 12 level.
# Key written first.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Units", "The becquerel is the SI unit of:",
      ["Radioactivity", "Luminous intensity", "Magnetic flux", "Electric charge"],
      "One becquerel is one nucleus decaying per second. Luminous intensity is measured in candela, magnetic flux in weber and charge in coulomb."),
    S(1, 2, "Heat", "A bimetallic strip bends when it is heated because its two metals:",
      ["Expand by different amounts", "Conduct electricity differently", "Have different colours", "Melt at the same temperature"],
      "Two metals with different coefficients of expansion, such as brass and iron, are joined. On heating one lengthens more, so the strip curls. Thermostats and fire alarms use this."),
    S(1, 1, "Light", "A simple periscope used in trenches and submarines works by:",
      ["Reflection from two plane mirrors", "Refraction through a convex lens", "Dispersion by a prism", "Diffraction at a narrow slit"],
      "Two plane mirrors set parallel to each other, each at 45° to the tube, turn the light twice, so the viewer sees over an obstacle."),
    S(1, 2, "Units", "The electron volt (eV) is a unit of:",
      ["Energy", "Potential difference", "Electric charge", "Electric current"],
      "One electron volt is the energy an electron gains when accelerated through a potential difference of 1 volt, about 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ joule. Despite the word volt, it measures energy."),
    S(1, 2, "Motion", "A stone is dropped from rest from a tall tower. Taking g = 10 m/s² and ignoring air resistance, how far does it fall in the first 2 seconds?",
      ["20 m", "10 m", "40 m", "5 m"],
      "s = ut + ½gt² = 0 + ½ × 10 × 2² = 20 m. 40 m forgets the ½, and 5 m is the distance in the first second only."),
    S(1, 2, "Electricity", "Three resistors of 2 Ω, 3 Ω and 6 Ω are connected in parallel. What is their equivalent resistance?",
      ["1 Ω", "11 Ω", "6 Ω", "2 Ω"],
      "In parallel, 1/R = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 3/6 + 2/6 + 1/6 = 1, so R = 1 Ω. 11 Ω is the series total. A parallel combination is always less than its smallest resistor."),
    S(1, 1, "Sound", "Sound waves with a frequency below 20 Hz, which humans cannot hear, are called:",
      ["Infrasonic waves", "Ultrasonic waves", "Supersonic waves", "Audible waves"],
      "The human ear hears roughly 20 Hz to 20 kHz. Below that range is infrasound (made by earthquakes and elephants); above it is ultrasound. Supersonic describes a speed faster than sound, not a frequency."),
    S(1, 2, "Light", "A ray of light passes through the optical centre of a thin lens. What happens to it?",
      ["It goes straight on without bending", "It passes through the principal focus", "It is reflected back", "It becomes parallel to the principal axis"],
      "Near the optical centre the two faces of a thin lens are nearly parallel, so the ray goes through undeviated. This is one of the three standard rays used to draw lens images."),
    S(1, 2, "Work and energy", "A force acts on a body at right angles to the direction in which it moves. How much work does the force do?",
      ["Zero", "Maximum", "Equal to force times distance", "Negative"],
      "Work = F × d × cos θ. At 90 degrees, cos θ = 0, so no work is done. That is why the force on a satellite in circular orbit does no work."),
    S(1, 2, "Heat", "At what temperature does water have its greatest density?",
      ["4 °C", "0 °C", "100 °C", "-4 °C"],
      "Water contracts as it cools to 4 °C and then expands again down to 0 °C. So a lake freezes from the top, and water at 4 °C stays at the bottom where fish survive the winter."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Light", "A mirage seen on a hot road is caused by:",
      ["Refraction and total internal reflection in layers of hot air", "Dispersion of light by dust", "Diffraction of light round the vehicles", "Reflection of light from water on the road"],
      "Air just above the hot road is less dense, so light from the sky bends more and more until it is totally reflected upward. The eye sees an image of the sky that looks like water; there is no water."),
    S(2, 2, "Surface tension", "A clean steel needle placed gently on still water can float. This is due to:",
      ["Surface tension", "Viscosity", "Capillarity", "Osmosis"],
      "The water surface behaves like a stretched skin because of surface tension, and it can support the needle even though steel is denser than water. A drop of soap lowers the surface tension and the needle sinks."),
    S(2, 1, "Light", "A ray of light strikes a plane mirror with an angle of incidence of 30°. What is the angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray?",
      ["60°", "30°", "90°", "120°"],
      "The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence, 30°. Both are measured from the normal, so the angle between the two rays is 30° + 30° = 60°."),
    S(2, 2, "Momentum", "The mass of a moving body is doubled and its velocity is halved. What happens to its momentum?",
      ["It stays the same", "It doubles", "It halves", "It becomes four times as large"],
      "Momentum p = mv. (2m) × (v/2) = mv, so it is unchanged. Its kinetic energy, ½mv², would halve."),
    S(2, 1, "Work and energy", "What is the kinetic energy of a 4 kg ball moving at 5 m/s?",
      ["50 J", "20 J", "100 J", "10 J"],
      "KE = ½mv² = ½ × 4 × 5² = 50 J. 20 J is the momentum value mv (in kg m/s, not joules), and 100 J forgets the ½."),
    S(2, 2, "Electricity", "An electric bulb is rated 60 W, 240 V. What is the resistance of its filament when it is working?",
      ["960 Ω", "4 Ω", "14400 Ω", "0.25 Ω"],
      "P = V²/R, so R = V²/P = 240 × 240 / 60 = 57,600 / 60 = 960 Ω. 0.25 is the current in amperes (60/240), 4 is 240/60 and 14400 is 240 × 60."),
    S(2, 2, "Electricity", "An ammeter is connected in a circuit:",
      ["In series, and has a very low resistance", "In parallel, and has a very low resistance", "In series, and has a very high resistance", "In parallel, and has a very high resistance"],
      "All the current must pass through an ammeter, so it goes in series, and its resistance must be very low so it does not change the current. A voltmeter goes in parallel with a very high resistance."),
    S(2, 1, "Sound", "Two astronauts on the Moon cannot hear each other speak directly because:",
      ["Sound needs a medium to travel", "The Moon's gravity is weak", "The Moon is too cold", "Their voices are too low"],
      "The Moon has almost no atmosphere, and sound is a mechanical wave that needs a medium. Astronauts talk by radio, since radio waves are electromagnetic and cross a vacuum."),
    S(2, 1, "Light", "An object is placed at the centre of curvature of a concave mirror. The image formed is:",
      ["Real, inverted and the same size as the object", "Virtual, erect and magnified", "Real, inverted and diminished", "Virtual, erect and diminished"],
      "Rays from an object at C come back through C, so the image forms at C itself, real, inverted and of the same size. Beyond C the image is real and diminished; between F and the mirror it is virtual, erect and magnified."),
    S(2, 1, "Sound", "During a storm we see the lightning before we hear the thunder because:",
      ["Light travels much faster than sound", "Thunder is produced after the lightning", "Sound is absorbed by clouds", "Our eyes react faster than our ears"],
      "Both are produced together, but light travels at about 3 × 10⁸ m/s and sound at about 340 m/s in air. Counting the seconds between flash and sound gives the distance: about 3 seconds per kilometre."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Waves", "Which of these waves cannot be polarised?",
      ["Sound waves in air", "Light waves", "Radio waves", "X-rays"],
      "Only transverse waves can be polarised. Sound in air is longitudinal; light, radio waves and X-rays are transverse electromagnetic waves."),
    S(0, 1, "Electromagnetic waves", "The remote control of a television usually sends signals using:",
      ["Infrared rays", "Ultraviolet rays", "X-rays", "Gamma rays"],
      "Most TV remotes have an infrared LED that sends coded pulses to a sensor on the set. Ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays are harmful in large doses and are not used for this."),
]
